"GetSecurityInfo function (aclapi.h) - Win32 apps | Microsoft Learn (https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/api/aclapi/nf-aclapi-getsecurityinfo)\nciteturn43view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/api/aclapi/nf-aclapi-getsecurityinfo\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 173\nL0: 【0†Skip to main content】 【1†Skip to Ask Learn chat experience】\nL1: \nL2: This browser is no longer supported.\nL3: \nL4: Upgrade to Microsoft Edge to take advantage of the latest features, security updates, and technical support.\nL5: \nL6: 【2†Download Microsoft Edge†go.microsoft.com】 【3†More info about Internet Explorer and Microsoft Edge】\nL7: \nL8: Table of contents Exit editor mode\nL9: \nL10: Ask Learn Ask Learn\nL11: \nL12: Reading mode Table of contents 【1†Read in English】 Add Add to Plans 【4†Edit†github.com】\nL13: \nL14: * * *\nL15: \nL16: Copy Markdown Print\nL17: \nL18: * * *\nL19: \nL20: Note\nL21: Access to this page requires authorization. You can try 【1†signing in】 or changing directories.\nL22: \nL23: Access to this page requires authorization. You can try changing directories.\nL24: # GetSecurityInfo function (aclapi.h)\nL25: \nL26: Feedback\nL27: \nL28: Summarize this article for me\nL29: \nL30: ## In this article\nL31: \nL32: The GetSecurityInfo function retrieves a copy of the 【5†security descriptor】 for an object specified by a handle.\nL33: ## Syntax\nL34: \nL35:     `DWORD GetSecurityInfo(\nL36:       [in]            HANDLE               handle,\nL37:       [in]            SE_OBJECT_TYPE       ObjectType,\nL38:       [in]            SECURITY_INFORMATION SecurityInfo,\nL39:       [out, optional] PSID                 *ppsidOwner,\nL40:       [out, optional] PSID                 *ppsidGroup,\nL41:       [out, optional] PACL                 *ppDacl,\nL42:       [out, optional] PACL                 *ppSacl,\nL43:       [out, optional] PSECURITY_DESCRIPTOR *ppSecurityDescriptor\nL44:     );\nL45:     `\nL46: ## Parameters\nL47: \nL48: `[in] handle`\nL49: \nL50: A handle to the object from which to retrieve security information.\nL51: \nL52: `[in] ObjectType`\nL53: \nL54: 【6†SE_OBJECT_TYPE】 enumeration value that indicates the type of object.\nL55: \nL56: `[in] SecurityInfo`\nL57: \nL58: A set of bit flags that indicate the type of security information to retrieve. This parameter can be a combination of the 【7†SECURITY_INFORMATION】 bit flags.\nL59: \nL60: `[out, optional] ppsidOwner`\nL61: A pointer to a variable that receives a pointer to the owner SID in the security descriptor returned in ppSecurityDescriptor. The returned pointer is valid only if you set the OWNER_SECURITY_INFORMATION flag. This parameter can be NULL if you do not need the owner SID.\nL62: \nL63: `[out, optional] ppsidGroup`\nL64: A pointer to a variable that receives a pointer to the primary group SID in the returned 【5†security descriptor】. The returned pointer is valid only if you set the GROUP_SECURITY_INFORMATION flag. This parameter can be NULL if you do not need the group SID.\nL65: \nL66: `[out, optional] ppDacl`\nL67: A pointer to a variable that receives a pointer to the DACL in the returned security descriptor. The returned pointer is valid only if you set the DACL_SECURITY_INFORMATION flag. This parameter can be NULL if you do not need the DACL.\nL68: \nL69: `[out, optional] ppSacl`\nL70: \nL71: A pointer to a variable that receives a pointer to the SACL in the returned security descriptor. The returned pointer is valid only if you set the SACL_SECURITY_INFORMATION flag. This parameter can be NULL if you do not need the SACL.\nL72: `[out, optional] ppSecurityDescriptor`\nL73: \nL74: A pointer to a variable that receives a pointer to the security descriptor of the object. When you have finished using the pointer, free the returned buffer by calling the 【8†LocalFree】 function.\nL75: \nL76: This parameter is required if any one of the ppsidOwner, ppsidGroup, ppDacl, or ppSacl parameters is not NULL.\nL77: ## Return value\nL78: \nL79: If the function succeeds, the return value is ERROR_SUCCESS.\nL80: \nL81: If the function fails, the return value is a nonzero error code defined in WinError.h.\nL82: ## Remarks\nL83: \nL84: If the ppsidOwner, ppsidGroup, ppDacl, and ppSacl parameters are non-NULL, and the SecurityInfo parameter specifies that they be retrieved from the object, those parameters will point to the corresponding parameters in the security descriptor returned in ppSecurityDescriptor.\nL85: To read the owner, group, or DACL from the object's security descriptor, the calling process must have been granted READ_CONTROL access when the handle was opened. To get READ_CONTROL access, the caller must be the owner of the object or the object's DACL must grant the access.\nL86: To read the SACL from the security descriptor, the calling process must have been granted ACCESS_SYSTEM_SECURITY access when the handle was opened. The proper way to get this access is to enable the SE_SECURITY_NAME privilege in the caller's current token, open the handle for ACCESS_SYSTEM_SECURITY access, and then disable the privilege. For information about the security implications of enabling privileges, see 【9†Running with Special Privileges】.\nL87: You can use the GetSecurityInfo function with the following types of objects:\nL88:   * Local or remote files or directories on an NTFS file system\nL89:   * Named pipes\nL90:   * Local or remote printers\nL91:   * Local or remote Windows services\nL92:   * Network shares\nL93:   * Registry keys\nL94:   * Semaphores, events, mutexes, and waitable timers\nL95:   * Processes, threads, jobs, and file-mapping objects\nL96:   * Interactive service window stations and desktops\nL97:   * Directory service objects\nL98: This function does not handle race conditions. If your thread calls this function at the approximate time that another thread changes the object's security descriptor, then this function could fail.\nL99: #### Examples\nL100: \nL101: For an example that uses this function, see 【10†Finding the Owner of a File Object】.\nL102: \nL103: ## Requirements\nL104: \nL105: Requirement  | Value\nL106: --- | ---\nL107: Minimum supported client  | Windows XP [desktop apps | UWP apps]\nL108: Minimum supported server  | Windows Server 2003 [desktop apps | UWP apps]\nL109: Target Platform  | Windows\nL110: Header  | aclapi.h\nL111: Library  | Advapi32.lib\nL112: DLL  | Advapi32.dll\nL113: ## See also\nL114: \nL115: 【11†ACL】\nL116: \nL117: 【12†Access Control Overview】\nL118: \nL119: 【13†Basic Access Control Functions】\nL120: \nL121: 【14†GetNamedSecurityInfo】\nL122: \nL123: 【8†LocalFree】\nL124: \nL125: 【15†Privilege Constants】\nL126: \nL127: 【16†SECURITY_DESCRIPTOR】\nL128: \nL129: 【7†SECURITY_INFORMATION】\nL130: \nL131: 【6†SE_OBJECT_TYPE】\nL132: \nL133: 【17†SID】\nL134: \nL135: 【18†SetNamedSecurityInfo】\nL136: \nL137: 【19†SetSecurityInfo】\nL138: \nL139: * * *\nL140: \nL141: ## Feedback\nL142: \nL143: Was this page helpful?\nL144: \nL145: Yes No No\nL146: \nL147: Need help with this topic?\nL148: \nL149: Want to try using Ask Learn to clarify or guide you through this topic?\nL150: \nL151: Ask Learn Ask Learn\nL152: \nL153: Suggest a fix?\nL154: \nL155: * * *\nL156: ## Additional resources\nL157: \nL158: * * *\nL159: \nL160:   * Last updated on 2021-10-13\nL161: \nL162: ### In this article\nL163: \nL164: Was this page helpful?\nL165: \nL166: Need help with this topic?\nL167: \nL168: Want to try using Ask Learn to clarify or guide you through this topic?\nL169: \nL170: Ask Learn Ask Learn\nL171: \nL172: Suggest a fix?\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAutomatic Propagation of Inheritable ACEs - Win32 apps | Microsoft Learn (https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/secauthz/automatic-propagation-of-inheritable-aces)\nciteturn43view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/secauthz/automatic-propagation-of-inheritable-aces\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 85\nL0: 【0†Skip to main content】 【1†Skip to Ask Learn chat experience】\nL1: \nL2: This browser is no longer supported.\nL3: \nL4: Upgrade to Microsoft Edge to take advantage of the latest features, security updates, and technical support.\nL5: \nL6: 【2†Download Microsoft Edge†go.microsoft.com】 【3†More info about Internet Explorer and Microsoft Edge】\nL7: \nL8: Table of contents Exit editor mode\nL9: \nL10: Ask Learn Ask Learn\nL11: \nL12: Reading mode Table of contents 【1†Read in English】 Add Add to Plans 【4†Edit†github.com】\nL13: \nL14: * * *\nL15: \nL16: Copy Markdown Print\nL17: \nL18: * * *\nL19: \nL20: Note\nL21: Access to this page requires authorization. You can try 【1†signing in】 or changing directories.\nL22: \nL23: Access to this page requires authorization. You can try changing directories.\nL24: # Automatic Propagation of Inheritable ACEs\nL25: \nL26: Feedback\nL27: \nL28: Summarize this article for me\nL29: ## In this article\nL30: \nL31: The 【5†SetNamedSecurityInfo】 and 【6†SetSecurityInfo】 functions support automatic propagation of inheritable 【7†access control entries】 (ACEs). For example, if you use these functions to add an inheritable ACE to a directory in an NTFS, the system applies the ACE as appropriate to the 【7†access control lists】 (ACLs) of any existing subdirectories or files.\nL32: Directly applied ACEs have precedence over inherited ACEs. The system implements this precedence by placing directly applied ACEs ahead of inherited ACEs in a 【8†discretionary access control list】 (DACL). When you call the 【5†SetNamedSecurityInfo】 and 【6†SetSecurityInfo】 functions to set the security information of an object, the system imposes the current inheritance model on the ACLs of all objects in the hierarchy below the target object.\nL33: For objects that have been converted to the current inheritance model, the SE_DACL_AUTO_INHERITED and SE_SACL_AUTO_INHERITED bits are set in the control field of the 【9†security descriptor】 of the object.\nL34: When you build a new security descriptor that reflects the current inheritance model, care is taken not to change the semantics of the security descriptor. As such, allow and deny ACEs are never moved in relation to one another. If such movement is needed (for instance to place all noninherited ACEs at the front of an ACL), the ACL is marked as protected to prevent the semantic change.\nL35: \nL36: The system uses the following rules when propagating inherited ACEs to child objects:\nL37:   * If a child object with no DACL inherits an ACE, the result is a child object with a DACL that contains only the inherited ACE.\nL38:   * If a child object with an empty DACL inherits an ACE, the result is a child object with a DACL that contains only the inherited ACE.\nL39:   * If you remove an inheritable ACE from a parent object, automatic inheritance removes any copies of the ACE that were inherited by child objects.\nL40:   * If automatic inheritance results in the removal of all ACEs from the DACL of a child object, the child object has an empty DACL rather than no DACL.\nL41: These rules can have the unexpected result of converting an object with no DACL to an object with an empty DACL. An object with no DACL allows full access, but an object with an empty DACL allows no access. As an example of how these rules can create an empty DACL, suppose you add an inheritable ACE to the root object of a tree of objects. Automatic inheritance propagates the inheritable ACE to all the objects in the tree. Child objects that started with no DACL now have a DACL with the inherited ACE.\nL42: If you remove the inheritable ACE from the root object, the system automatically propagates the change to the child objects. Child objects that started with no DACL (allowing full access) now have an empty DACL (allowing no access).\nL43: To ensure that a child object with no DACL is not affected by inheritable ACEs, set the SE_DACL_PROTECTED flag in the security descriptor of the object.\nL44: \nL45: For information about how to properly create a DACL, see 【10†Creating a DACL】.\nL46: \nL47: \nL48: \nL49: \nL50: \nL51: * * *\nL52: ## Feedback\nL53: \nL54: Was this page helpful?\nL55: \nL56: Yes No No\nL57: \nL58: Need help with this topic?\nL59: \nL60: Want to try using Ask Learn to clarify or guide you through this topic?\nL61: \nL62: Ask Learn Ask Learn\nL63: \nL64: Suggest a fix?\nL65: \nL66: * * *\nL67: \nL68: ## Additional resources\nL69: \nL70: * * *\nL71: \nL72:   * Last updated on 2021-01-07\nL73: \nL74: ### In this article\nL75: \nL76: Was this page helpful?\nL77: \nL78: Need help with this topic?\nL79: \nL80: Want to try using Ask Learn to clarify or guide you through this topic?\nL81: \nL82: Ask Learn Ask Learn\nL83: \nL84: Suggest a fix?\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSECURITY_DESCRIPTOR_CONTROL (Winnt.h) - Win32 apps | Microsoft Learn (https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/secauthz/security-descriptor-control)\nciteturn43view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/secauthz/security-descriptor-control\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 168\nL0: 【0†Skip to main content】 【1†Skip to Ask Learn chat experience】\nL1: \nL2: This browser is no longer supported.\nL3: \nL4: Upgrade to Microsoft Edge to take advantage of the latest features, security updates, and technical support.\nL5: \nL6: 【2†Download Microsoft Edge†go.microsoft.com】 【3†More info about Internet Explorer and Microsoft Edge】\nL7: \nL8: Table of contents Exit editor mode\nL9: \nL10: Ask Learn Ask Learn\nL11: \nL12: Reading mode Table of contents 【1†Read in English】 Add Add to Plans 【4†Edit†github.com】\nL13: \nL14: * * *\nL15: \nL16: Copy Markdown Print\nL17: \nL18: * * *\nL19: \nL20: Note\nL21: Access to this page requires authorization. You can try 【1†signing in】 or changing directories.\nL22: \nL23: Access to this page requires authorization. You can try changing directories.\nL24: # SECURITY_DESCRIPTOR_CONTROL\nL25: \nL26: Feedback\nL27: \nL28: Summarize this article for me\nL29: \nL30: ## In this article\nL31: \nL32: The SECURITY_DESCRIPTOR_CONTROL data type is a set of bit flags that qualify the meaning of a 【5†security descriptor】 or its components. Each security descriptor has a Control member that stores the SECURITY_DESCRIPTOR_CONTROL bits.\nL33: \nL34:     `typedef WORD SECURITY_DESCRIPTOR_CONTROL, *PSECURITY_DESCRIPTOR_CONTROL;\nL35:     `\nL36: ## Remarks\nL37: \nL38: To get the control bits of a security descriptor, call the 【6†GetSecurityDescriptorControl】 function. To set the control bits of a security descriptor, use the functions for modifying security descriptors. For a list of these functions, see the See Also section.\nL39: \nL40: Applications can use the 【7†SetSecurityDescriptorControl】 function to set the control bits that relate to automatic inheritance of ACEs.\nL41: The control value retrieved by the 【6†GetSecurityDescriptorControl】 function can include a combination of the following SECURITY_DESCRIPTOR_CONTROL bit flags.\nL42: Value  | Meaning\nL43: --- | ---\nL44: SE_DACL_AUTO_INHERIT_REQ\nL45: 0x0100\nL46:  | Indicates a required 【5†security descriptor】 in which the 【8†discretionary access control list】 (DACL) is set up to support automatic propagation of inheritable 【9†access control entries】 (ACEs) to existing child objects.\nL47: For 【9†access control lists】 (ACLs) that support auto inheritance, this bit is always set. Protected servers can call the 【10†ConvertToAutoInheritPrivateObjectSecurity】 function to convert a security descriptor and set this flag.\nL48: SE_DACL_AUTO_INHERITED\nL49: 0x0400\nL50:  | Indicates a security descriptor in which the 【8†discretionary access control list】 (DACL) is set up to support automatic propagation of inheritable 【9†access control entries】 (ACEs) to existing child objects.\nL51: For 【9†access control lists】 (ACLs) that support auto inheritance, this bit is always set. Protected servers can call the 【10†ConvertToAutoInheritPrivateObjectSecurity】 function to convert a security descriptor and set this flag.\nL52: SE_DACL_DEFAULTED\nL53: 0x0008\nL54:  | Indicates a security descriptor with a default DACL. For example, if the creator an object does not specify a DACL, the object receives the default DACL from the 【9†access token】 of the creator. This flag can affect how the system treats the DACL with respect to ACE inheritance. The system ignores this flag if the SE_DACL_PRESENT flag is not set.\nL55: This flag is used to determine how the final DACL on the object is to be computed and is not stored physically in the security descriptor control of the securable object.\nL56: To set this flag, use the 【11†SetSecurityDescriptorDacl】 function.\nL57: SE_DACL_PRESENT\nL58: 0x0004\nL59:  | Indicates a security descriptor that has a DACL. If this flag is not set, or if this flag is set and the DACL is NULL, the security descriptor allows full access to everyone.\nL60: This flag is used to hold the security information specified by a caller until the security descriptor is associated with a securable object. After the security descriptor is associated with a securable object, the SE_DACL_PRESENT flag is always set in the security descriptor control.\nL61: To set this flag, use the 【11†SetSecurityDescriptorDacl】 function.\nL62: SE_DACL_PROTECTED\nL63: 0x1000\nL64:  | Prevents the DACL of the security descriptor from being modified by inheritable ACEs. To set this flag, use the 【7†SetSecurityDescriptorControl】 function.\nL65: SE_GROUP_DEFAULTED\nL66: 0x0002\nL67:  | Indicates that the 【5†security identifier】 (SID) of the security descriptor group was provided by a default mechanism. This flag can be used by a resource manager to identify objects whose security descriptor group was set by a default mechanism. To set this flag, use the 【12†SetSecurityDescriptorGroup】 function.\nL68: SE_OWNER_DEFAULTED\nL69: 0x0001\nL70:  | Indicates that the SID of the owner of the security descriptor was provided by a default mechanism. This flag can be used by a resource manager to identify objects whose owner was set by a default mechanism. To set this flag, use the 【13†SetSecurityDescriptorOwner】 function.\nL71: SE_RM_CONTROL_VALID\nL72: 0x4000\nL73:  | Indicates that the resource manager control is valid.\nL74: SE_SACL_AUTO_INHERIT_REQ\nL75: 0x0200\nL76:  | Indicates a required security descriptor in which the 【5†system access control list】 (SACL) is set up to support automatic propagation of inheritable ACEs to existing child objects.\nL77: The system sets this bit when it performs the automatic inheritance algorithm for the object and its existing child objects. To convert a security descriptor and set this flag, protected servers can call the 【10†ConvertToAutoInheritPrivateObjectSecurity】 function.\nL78: SE_SACL_AUTO_INHERITED\nL79: 0x0800\nL80:  | Indicates a security descriptor in which the 【5†system access control list】 (SACL) is set up to support automatic propagation of inheritable ACEs to existing child objects.\nL81: The system sets this bit when it performs the automatic inheritance algorithm for the object and its existing child objects. To convert a security descriptor and set this flag, protected servers can call the 【10†ConvertToAutoInheritPrivateObjectSecurity】 function.\nL82: SE_SACL_DEFAULTED\nL83: 0x0020\nL84:  | A default mechanism, rather than the original 【14†provider】 of the security descriptor, provided the SACL. This flag can affect how the system treats the SACL, with respect to ACE inheritance. The system ignores this flag if the SE_SACL_PRESENT flag is not set. To set this flag, use the 【15†SetSecurityDescriptorSacl】 function.\nL85: SE_SACL_PRESENT\nL86: 0x0010\nL87:  | Indicates a security descriptor that has a SACL. To set this flag, use the 【15†SetSecurityDescriptorSacl】 function.\nL88: SE_SACL_PROTECTED\nL89: 0x2000\nL90:  | Prevents the SACL of the security descriptor from being modified by inheritable ACEs. To set this flag, use the 【7†SetSecurityDescriptorControl】 function.\nL91: SE_SELF_RELATIVE\nL92: 0x8000\nL93:  | Indicates a 【5†self-relative security descriptor】. If this flag is not set, the security descriptor is in absolute format. For more information, see 【16†Absolute and Self-Relative Security Descriptors】.\nL94: ## Requirements\nL95: \nL96: Requirement  | Value\nL97: --- | ---\nL98: Minimum supported client\nL99:  | Windows XP [desktop apps only]\nL100: Minimum supported server\nL101:  | Windows Server 2003 [desktop apps only]\nL102: Header\nL103:  |\nL104: \nL105: Winnt.h (include Windows.h)\nL106: ## See also\nL107: \nL108: 【17†Low-level Access Control】\nL109: \nL110: 【18†Basic Access Control Structures】\nL111: \nL112: 【10†ConvertToAutoInheritPrivateObjectSecurity】\nL113: \nL114: 【6†GetSecurityDescriptorControl】\nL115: \nL116: 【19†GetSecurityDescriptorDacl】\nL117: \nL118: 【20†GetSecurityDescriptorGroup】\nL119: \nL120: 【21†GetSecurityDescriptorOwner】\nL121: \nL122: 【22†GetSecurityDescriptorSacl】\nL123: \nL124: 【7†SetSecurityDescriptorControl】\nL125: \nL126: 【11†SetSecurityDescriptorDacl】\nL127: \nL128: 【12†SetSecurityDescriptorGroup】\nL129: \nL130: 【13†SetSecurityDescriptorOwner】\nL131: \nL132: 【15†SetSecurityDescriptorSacl】\nL133: \nL134: * * *\nL135: ## Feedback\nL136: \nL137: Was this page helpful?\nL138: \nL139: Yes No No\nL140: \nL141: Need help with this topic?\nL142: \nL143: Want to try using Ask Learn to clarify or guide you through this topic?\nL144: \nL145: Ask Learn Ask Learn\nL146: \nL147: Suggest a fix?\nL148: \nL149: * * *\nL150: \nL151: ## Additional resources\nL152: \nL153: * * *\nL154: \nL155:   * Last updated on 2021-01-07\nL156: \nL157: ### In this article\nL158: \nL159: Was this page helpful?\nL160: \nL161: Need help with this topic?\nL162: \